pg:CO1 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Open a PDF with PyPDF, count its pages, extract text and images, and explain why the text comes out in rows and with gaps, and what must be installed for images"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can open a PDF with PyPDF, count its pages, extract text and images, and explain why the text comes out in rows and with gaps, and what must be installed for images, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter17.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:CO2 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Build a new PDF from pages of others with append and merge, choose between a tuple and a list of page indexes, rotate pages, add blank pages, merge a watermark or an overlay, and encrypt and decrypt with the right password type"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can build a new PDF from pages of others with append and merge, choose between a tuple and a list of page indexes, rotate pages, add blank pages, merge a watermark or an overlay, and encrypt and decrypt with the right password type, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter17.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:CO3 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Read and write Word documents with Python-Docx: paragraphs, runs, styles and their linked form, tri-state run attributes, headings, breaks and pictures"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can read and write Word documents with Python-Docx: paragraphs, runs, styles and their linked form, tri-state run attributes, headings, breaks and pictures, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter17.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:CO4 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Write the file-combining program of Project 12 so that it runs twice with the same result, and plan the invitation and password programs without the book's downloadable files"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can write the file-combining program of Project 12 so that it runs twice with the same result, and plan the invitation and password programs without the book's downloadable files, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter17.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:CO5 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Analyze which tasks on PDF and Word files can be automated and which need a person, and where the chapter's printed names and outputs differ from current package releases"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can analyze which tasks on PDF and Word files can be automated and which need a person, and where the chapter's printed names and outputs differ from current package releases, at the analyze level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter17.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze  .
pg:Q1 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A PdfWriter is told writer.append('a.pdf', (0, 5, 2)). Which page indexes are copied?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q1_O0, pg:Q1_O1, pg:Q1_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q1_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "A tuple of three integers is read like range(0, 5, 2), so every second page is taken, not every page." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "A tuple is a range, not a list of the pages to copy; 5 is the stop value and is never copied." .
pg:Q1_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "[0, 1, 2, 3, 4]" .
pg:Q1_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "[0, 2, 4]" .
pg:Q1_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "[0, 5, 2]" .
pg:Q2 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Writing writer.append('a.pdf', [0, 5]) for a six-page file copies which pages?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q2_O0, pg:Q2_O1, pg:Q2_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q2_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "That is what the tuple (0, 5) does; a list names the pages one by one." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "append() accepts a list, and in the library run it copied two pages." .
pg:Q2_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Page index 0 and page index 5 only" .
pg:Q2_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Page indexes 0 to 4" .
pg:Q2_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Nothing, because a list is not accepted" .
pg:Q3 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "In Project 12 the call is writer.append(name, (1, len(reader.pages))). What does it add for a PDF of one page?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q3_O0, pg:Q3_O1, pg:Q3_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q3_O2 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO4 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The range starts at index 1, so the first page is always left out and a one-page file gives an empty range." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "An empty range is not an error; the library run combined a one-page file with two others without one." .
pg:Q3_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Its only page" .
pg:Q3_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Nothing, because it raises IndexError" .
pg:Q3_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "No pages at all" .
pg:Q4 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Project 12, with its name error corrected, is run twice in the same folder. Why does the second combined.pdf have more pages?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q4_O0, pg:Q4_O1, pg:Q4_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q4_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO4 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The writer is a new object on every run and keeps nothing." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "os.listdir() returns each name once; the extra pages come from the output file being listed." .
pg:Q4_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "append() remembers the pages of the earlier run" .
pg:Q4_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "combined.pdf is itself a .pdf file in the folder and is read as an input" .
pg:Q4_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "os.listdir() returns each file twice" .
pg:Q5 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "What does page.merge_page(watermark_page, over=False) do?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q5_O0, pg:Q5_O1, pg:Q5_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q5_O2 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Understand ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Putting it on top is over=True, which the pypdf documentation calls a stamp or overlay." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The contents are combined, not replaced." .
pg:Q5_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Places the other page on top of the existing content" .
pg:Q5_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Replaces the page's content with the other page" .
pg:Q5_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Places the other page underneath the page's existing content" .
pg:Q6 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "After reader.decrypt('an incorrect password'), what is the name of the value it returns?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q6_O0, pg:Q6_O1, pg:Q6_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q6_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Remember ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "That name is returned when the owner password was the one given." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The error is raised when pages are read before a successful decrypt, not by decrypt() itself." .
pg:Q6_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'NOT_DECRYPTED'" .
pg:Q6_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'OWNER_PASSWORD'" .
pg:Q6_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It raises FileNotDecryptedError instead" .
pg:Q7 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A paragraph holds plain text, then a bold word, then plain text, then an italic word. How many Run objects does it have?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q7_O0, pg:Q7_O1, pg:Q7_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q7_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Understand ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO3 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "A run ends whenever the style changes, and there are four stretches of text." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "A paragraph with one run would have a single style throughout." .
pg:Q7_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "2" .
pg:Q7_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "4" .
pg:Q7_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "1" .
pg:Q8 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A run's bold attribute is None. What decides whether its text is shown in bold?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q8_O0, pg:Q8_O1, pg:Q8_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q8_O2 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Understand ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO3 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "False, not None, switches bold off for good." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The text of a paragraph carries no styling; styling is held by runs and styles." .
pg:Q8_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Nothing; the text is never bold" .
pg:Q8_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The text of the paragraph" .
pg:Q8_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The style of the run" .
pg:Q9 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Which name applies the linked style whose paragraph form is 'Quote' to a Run?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q9_O0, pg:Q9_O1, pg:Q9_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q9_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO3 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The paragraph name was refused for a run with ValueError in the library run." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Python-Docx looks up a style by its name; 'Quote Run' is not one, and the linked style uses ' Char'." .
pg:Q9_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'Quote'" .
pg:Q9_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'Quote Char'" .
pg:Q9_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'Quote Run'" .
pg:Q10 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "pypdf 6.20.1 encrypts a PDF with writer.encrypt('pw') and no algorithm. What does the page's example show?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q10_O0, pg:Q10_O1, pg:Q10_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q10_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO5 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "That is what algorithm='AES-256' wrote; the default is RC4, which the documentation calls insecure." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The call succeeded and wrote an encrypted file." .
pg:Q10_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The older RC4 encryption, version 2 revision 3" .
pg:Q10_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "AES-256 encryption, version 5 revision 6" .
pg:Q10_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "No encryption, because an algorithm is required" .
pg:Q11 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "What does doc.add_heading('Top', 0) add to a Python-Docx document?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q11_O0, pg:Q11_O1, pg:Q11_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q11_O2 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Remember ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO3 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Level 0 gives Title, and Heading 1 is the first numbered heading." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Levels 0 to 9 are accepted; the error came at level 10." .
pg:Q11_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "A paragraph in a style called Heading 0" .
pg:Q11_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Nothing; it raises ValueError for level 0" .
pg:Q11_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "A paragraph in the Title style" .
pg:PdfFormat a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Pdf format"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter opens by saying that PDF and Word are binary files that also hold font, colour and layout information, so a program cannot simply pass their filenames to open(), and that a PDF is great for laying out text for people to print and read and is not easy to parse into plaintext (Sweigart, 2025). The PDF standard describes the file as a header, numbered objects, a cross-reference table and a trailer, and the text of a page as drawing commands (Adobe Systems, 2008)(Library of Congress, 2019)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_PdfFormat> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PdfFormat" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PdfFormat .
pg:FileStructure a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "File structure"@en ;
    skos:definition "A PDF file starts with a header line that names the version, is followed by numbered objects, and ends with a cross-reference table, a trailer and the line %%EOF; the pages, the fonts and the drawing commands of each page are such objects (Adobe Systems, 2008)(Library of Congress, 2019). The chapter does not describe the file, only the PyPDF objects that stand for it (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_FileStructure> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FileStructure" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PdfFormat .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FileStructure .
pg:HeaderAndTrailer a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Header and trailer"@en ;
    skos:definition "A PDF file begins with a header line such as %PDF-1.4 and ends with the keyword startxref, the byte offset of the cross-reference table and the marker %%EOF; the standard says a reader finds the table by reading from the end of the file (Adobe Systems, 2008)(Library of Congress, 2019). The chapter never opens a PDF as bytes, since PyPDF does it (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_HeaderAndTrailer> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-HeaderAndTrailer" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FileStructure .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:HeaderAndTrailer .
pg:CrossReferenceTable a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Cross reference table"@en ;
    skos:definition "The cross-reference table lists, for each numbered object of the file, the byte offset at which the object starts and whether it is in use; entry 0 is the head of the list of free objects (Adobe Systems, 2008). A reader that has the table can jump to any page without reading the whole file (Library of Congress, 2019)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_CrossReferenceTable> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CrossReferenceTable" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FileStructure .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CrossReferenceTable .
pg:ContentStreamText a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Content stream text"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter extracts the text of a page with extract_text(), and the PDF standard says the text of a page is a content stream of drawing commands: BT begins a text object, Tf selects a font and size, Td moves to a position, Tj shows a string and ET ends it (Sweigart, 2025)(Adobe Systems, 2008)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_ContentStreamText> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ContentStreamText" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FileStructure .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ContentStreamText .
pg:ProducerMetadata a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Producer metadata"@en ;
    skos:definition "A PDF may carry document information such as the producer and creation date; the chapter does not read metadata, and the pypdf documentation says the reader's metadata attribute gives it (Sweigart, 2025)(pypdf contributors, 2026). A file that a library writes records the library as its producer."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_ProducerMetadata> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ProducerMetadata" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FileStructure .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ProducerMetadata .
pg:TextAndLayout a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Text and layout"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter warns that PDFs are great for laying out text for people and are not easy to parse into plaintext, and lists the choices that extraction cannot make by itself: where paragraphs end and begin, whether page numbers, headers and footers are included, how tables are converted and how much whitespace is kept (Sweigart, 2025). The PDF standard places text with drawing commands at coordinates, in the order the stream gives them (Adobe Systems, 2008)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_TextAndLayout> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TextAndLayout" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PdfFormat .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TextAndLayout .
pg:TextOrderIsStreamOrder a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Text order is stream order"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's list of subjective decisions starts with where paragraphs end and begin and does not mention the order of the text (Sweigart, 2025). The standard lets a page draw its strings in any order, because each Td or Tm command says where the next string goes (Adobe Systems, 2008)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_TextOrderIsStreamOrder> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TextOrderIsStreamOrder" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TextAndLayout .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TextOrderIsStreamOrder .
pg:ScannedPageNoText a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Scanned page no text"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says PyPDF may make mistakes when extracting text and may even fail to open some PDFs, and does not say what a scanned document gives (Sweigart, 2025). A scan is an image on a page with no text commands, so there is nothing to extract; recognising the letters of a picture is a different task from the one extract_text() does."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_ScannedPageNoText> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ScannedPageNoText" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TextAndLayout .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ScannedPageNoText .
pg:RedactionByOverlay a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Redaction by overlay"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter shows how to lay one page over another with merge_page(), and a black rectangle drawn over text is the same kind of overlay (Sweigart, 2025). Wikipedia says that where text is redacted in a PDF by overlaying graphical elements, usually black rectangles, the original text remains in the file and can be uncovered by deleting the overlay (Wikipedia, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_RedactionByOverlay> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RedactionByOverlay" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TextAndLayout .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RedactionByOverlay .
pg:NoParagraphsInPdf a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "No paragraphs in pdf"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter asks where paragraphs in the PDF should end and begin, and answers that these are subjective decisions of the extraction (Sweigart, 2025). A PDF stores each string where it is drawn, so a paragraph exists only as a group of lines that a human sees (Adobe Systems, 2008)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_NoParagraphsInPdf> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-NoParagraphsInPdf" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TextAndLayout .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:NoParagraphsInPdf .
pg:PdfTextAndImages a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Pdf text and images"@en ;
    skos:definition "The first task of the PDF section is to read a PDF with PyPDF: open a PdfReader, loop over its pages, call extract_text() on each and add the strings together, then write the text to a file, with pdfminer.six as a fallback when PyPDF cannot open a file, and extract the images of each page and write them to files (Sweigart, 2025)(pypdf contributors, 2026). The chapter says PyPDF might make mistakes when extracting text and may fail to open some PDFs, and that cleaning the extracted text is boring and cannot easily be automated (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_PdfTextAndImages> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PdfTextAndImages" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PdfTextAndImages .
pg:ReadingPages a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Reading pages"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter opens a file in binary read mode, builds a PdfReader from it, takes len(reader.pages) and loops over reader.pages, calling extract_text() on each page and adding the string to a variable; page 1 of the PDF is pages[0] (Sweigart, 2025)(pypdf contributors, 2026). The pages attribute is a list-like collection of Page objects (pypdf contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_ReadingPages> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ReadingPages" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PdfTextAndImages .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ReadingPages .
pg:PdfReaderPages a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Pdf reader pages"@en ;
    skos:definition "After import pypdf the chapter opens the file in binary read mode and passes it to PdfReader; the pages attribute holds the Page objects, len(reader.pages) gives their number and the loop for page in reader.pages visits them in order (Sweigart, 2025)(pypdf contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_PdfReaderPages> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PdfReaderPages" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ReadingPages .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PdfReaderPages .
pg:PageIndexIsZeroBased a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Page index is zero based"@en ;
    skos:definition "Practice question 2 asks how to get the Page object for page 5, and the chapter counts page indexes from 0 when it says the tuple (0, 5) copies pages from page index 0 up to but not including page index 5 (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_PageIndexIsZeroBased> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PageIndexIsZeroBased" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ReadingPages .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PageIndexIsZeroBased .
pg:JoinedPagesNoSeparator a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Joined pages no separator"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's extraction listing sets text to an empty string and, for each page, adds the text of the page with +=, then writes the result to a file (Sweigart, 2025). extract_text() returns the text of one page without a trailing newline or a page marker (pypdf contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_JoinedPagesNoSeparator> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-JoinedPagesNoSeparator" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ReadingPages .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:JoinedPagesNoSeparator .
pg:ExtractionQuality a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Extraction quality"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says PyPDF can make mistakes, offers the pdfminer.six function extract_text() as a fallback for a file PyPDF cannot understand, and suggests a language model prompt to clean up hyphens, headers and paragraph breaks (Sweigart, 2025)(pdfminer.six contributors, 2026). The pypdf documentation lists extraction modes and says post-processing is outside the library (pypdf contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_ExtractionQuality> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ExtractionQuality" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PdfTextAndImages .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ExtractionQuality .
pg:LayoutMode a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Layout mode"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls extract_text() with no argument. The pypdf documentation shows extraction_mode='layout', which extracts text in a fixed-width format that closely adheres to the rendered layout of the source PDF (pypdf contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_LayoutMode> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LayoutMode" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ExtractionQuality .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LayoutMode .
pg:PdfminerAlternative a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Pdfminer alternative"@en ;
    skos:definition "When PyPDF cannot understand an unconventional file the chapter turns to pdfminer.high_level, whose extract_text() function takes the filename and returns the whole text as one string, written to a file (Sweigart, 2025)(pdfminer.six contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_PdfminerAlternative> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PdfminerAlternative" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ExtractionQuality .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PdfminerAlternative .
pg:DamagedInput a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Damaged input"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says PyPDF may fail to open some PDFs and that the author has not met one it could not open (Sweigart, 2025). The fallback it offers is pdfminer.six (Sweigart, 2025)(pdfminer.six contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_DamagedInput> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DamagedInput" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ExtractionQuality .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DamagedInput .
pg:TextPostProcessing a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Text post processing"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter prints the extracted text of a page with a newline at the end of every printed row and the word following split by a hyphen across two rows, and suggests a language model prompt that puts paragraphs on a single line and removes the hyphens and the headers (Sweigart, 2025). The pypdf documentation says post-processing is outside the scope of pypdf, calls it a natural language processing task and shows ligature replacement and dehyphenation as examples (pypdf contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_TextPostProcessing> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TextPostProcessing" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ExtractionQuality .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TextPostProcessing .
pg:ImageExtraction a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Image extraction"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter extracts images with the images attribute of a Page object, whose items have a name and the bytes in data, and writes each image to a file opened in binary write mode, putting a counter and the page number in front of the name (Sweigart, 2025)(pypdf contributors, 2026). The chapter's text does not say that the attribute needs another package (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_ImageExtraction> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ImageExtraction" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PdfTextAndImages .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ImageExtraction .
pg:ImagesNeedPillow a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Images need pillow"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's image listing loops over page.images and writes image.data to a file, and its text does not say that the attribute needs a second package (Sweigart, 2025). The pypdf installation page says that for image extraction you need Pillow, installed with the image extra (pypdf contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_ImagesNeedPillow> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ImagesNeedPillow" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ImageExtraction .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ImagesNeedPillow .
pg:ImageNameAndData a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Image name and data"@en ;
    skos:definition "Each item of page.images is an image object whose name attribute is a filename and whose data attribute holds the bytes of the file; the chapter writes the data to a file named from the counter, the page number and the name (Sweigart, 2025)(pypdf contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_ImageNameAndData> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ImageNameAndData" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ImageExtraction .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ImageNameAndData .
pg:ImageNamesRepeat a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Image names repeat"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter writes each image to a file named with a running number, the page number and the image's name, for example 0_page1_Im0.jpg, and does not say why the number is needed (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_ImageNamesRepeat> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ImageNamesRepeat" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ImageExtraction .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ImageNamesRepeat .
pg:PdfWriting a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Pdf writing"@en ;
    skos:definition "The second task is to create a new PDF from pages of existing ones: a PdfWriter starts empty, copies pages from files with append() and merge(), rotates them, adds blank pages, merges a watermark or overlay and encrypts the result, and PdfReader decrypts a protected file (Sweigart, 2025)(pypdf contributors, 2026). Project 12 puts the copying to work by combining the pages after the first of every PDF in a folder into one file (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_PdfWriting> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PdfWriting" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PdfWriting .
pg:PageSelection a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Page selection"@en ;
    skos:definition "A new PdfWriter has no pages; the chapter calls writer.append() with a filename and a tuple of two integers, which tells the writer to copy pages starting at page index 0 up to but not including index 5, and says a third integer is a step; a list names pages exactly (Sweigart, 2025)(pypdf contributors, 2026). The write() method saves the writer to a file opened in binary write mode (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_PageSelection> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PageSelection" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PdfWriting .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PageSelection .
pg:PdfWriterStartsEmpty a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Pdf writer starts empty"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter creates a new PDF with pypdf.PdfWriter(): the object starts with no pages and receives pages copied from other files with append() and merge(), never text typed in; write() saves it to a file opened in binary write mode (Sweigart, 2025)(pypdf contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_PdfWriterStartsEmpty> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PdfWriterStartsEmpty" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PageSelection .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PdfWriterStartsEmpty .
pg:AppendRangeTuple a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Append range tuple"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls writer.append('Recursion_Chapter1.pdf', (0, 5)) and explains that the tuple tells the PdfWriter to copy pages starting at page index 0, up to but not including page index 5 (Sweigart, 2025)(pypdf contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_AppendRangeTuple> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AppendRangeTuple" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PageSelection .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AppendRangeTuple .
pg:AppendStepTuple a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Append step tuple"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that a tuple of three integers adds a step to the range, as in range(), so that (0, 5, 2) copies every second page (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_AppendStepTuple> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AppendStepTuple" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PageSelection .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AppendStepTuple .
pg:AppendListOfPages a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Append list of pages"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that a list of integers selects exactly the pages it names, where a tuple selects a range, and the difference is the one between range(0, 5) and [0, 5] (Sweigart, 2025)(pypdf contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_AppendListOfPages> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AppendListOfPages" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PageSelection .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AppendListOfPages .
pg:AppendOutOfRange a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Append out of range"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's examples keep inside the pages of the file, and the sample chapter has 18 pages (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_AppendOutOfRange> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AppendOutOfRange" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PageSelection .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AppendOutOfRange .
pg:InsertAndMerge a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Insert and merge"@en ;
    skos:definition "The append() method adds copied pages at the end; to insert them before the end the chapter calls merge(), which has an additional integer argument that gives the position, and add_blank_page() and insert_blank_page() add a page whose size, the chapter says, is the same as that of the preceding page (Sweigart, 2025)(pypdf contributors, 2026). The chapter prints merge(2, 'Recursion_Chapter1.pdf', (0, 5)) (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_InsertAndMerge> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-InsertAndMerge" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PdfWriting .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:InsertAndMerge .
pg:MergeInsertsAtPosition a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Merge inserts at position"@en ;
    skos:definition "The merge() method has an additional integer argument that specifies the position at which the pages are inserted, so the pages that were at and after that position move later (Sweigart, 2025)(pypdf contributors, 2026). The pypdf documentation gives the keyword pages for the page selection (pypdf contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_MergeInsertsAtPosition> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MergeInsertsAtPosition" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:InsertAndMerge .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MergeInsertsAtPosition .
pg:MergePositionalTupleTrap a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Merge positional tuple trap"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter prints writer.merge(2, 'Recursion_Chapter1.pdf', (0, 5)) with the tuple as the third positional argument and shows no output (Sweigart, 2025). The signature of merge() in pypdf 6.20.1 is position, fileobj, outline_item, pages (pypdf contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_MergePositionalTupleTrap> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MergePositionalTupleTrap" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:InsertAndMerge .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MergePositionalTupleTrap .
pg:BlankPageSize a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Blank page size"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that the size of a new blank page will be the same as that of the preceding page, and creates one with add_blank_page() at the end and one with insert_blank_page(index=2) (Sweigart, 2025)(pypdf contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_BlankPageSize> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BlankPageSize" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:InsertAndMerge .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BlankPageSize .
pg:InsertBlankPageArguments a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Insert blank page arguments"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter writes insert_blank_page(index=2) with the keyword, and the signature of the method is width, height, index in that order (Sweigart, 2025)(pypdf contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_InsertBlankPageArguments> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-InsertBlankPageArguments" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:InsertAndMerge .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:InsertBlankPageArguments .
pg:PageEditing a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Page editing"@en ;
    skos:definition "Page objects are edited in place: rotate() turns a page by a multiple of 90 degrees, clockwise when positive, and merge_page() lays the content of one page on another, a watermark under the text or an overlay over it (Sweigart, 2025)(pypdf contributors, 2026). The chapter rotates every page of the sample chapter in a loop and applies a watermark PDF to pages of it (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_PageEditing> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PageEditing" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PdfWriting .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PageEditing .
pg:RotateMultiplesOf90 a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Rotate multiples of90"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter rotates pages with the rotate() method of Page objects: 90, 180 or 270 turn the page clockwise and -90, -180 or -270 counterclockwise (Sweigart, 2025)(pypdf contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_RotateMultiplesOf90> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RotateMultiplesOf90" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PageEditing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RotateMultiplesOf90 .
pg:RotationAccumulates a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Rotation accumulates"@en ;
    skos:definition "Practice question 4 asks how to rotate a page counterclockwise, and the answer in the chapter's own text is a negative angle (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_RotationAccumulates> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RotationAccumulates" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PageEditing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RotationAccumulates .
pg:WatermarkUnderlay a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Watermark underlay"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter merges a one-page watermark PDF into each page with merge_page() and the keyword over=False, so the watermark goes under the text, and notes that the default puts it over (Sweigart, 2025)(pypdf contributors, 2026). Figure 17-3 shows the result (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_WatermarkUnderlay> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WatermarkUnderlay" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PageEditing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WatermarkUnderlay .
pg:MergePageInPlace a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Merge page in place"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls page.merge_page(watermark_page, over=False) and then adds the page to the writer, without using a return value (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_MergePageInPlace> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MergePageInPlace" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PageEditing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MergePageInPlace .
pg:Protection a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Protection"@en ;
    skos:definition "PdfWriter.encrypt() accepts a password string and a string that selects the algorithm, and the chapter always uses 'AES-256'; a PdfReader reports is_encrypted and its decrypt() method returns a PasswordType object whose name is NOT_DECRYPTED, USER_PASSWORD or OWNER_PASSWORD (Sweigart, 2025)(pypdf contributors, 2026). A PDF can have a user password to view it and an owner password to set permissions (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_Protection> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Protection" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PdfWriting .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Protection .
pg:EncryptRc4Default a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Encrypt rc4 default"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says the encrypt() method accepts a password and a string that selects the algorithm and that it will always use AES-256; the call without an algorithm is not shown (Sweigart, 2025). The pypdf documentation names the algorithms that encrypt() offers (pypdf contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_EncryptRc4Default> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-EncryptRc4Default" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Protection .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:EncryptRc4Default .
pg:EncryptAes256 a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Encrypt aes256"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls writer.encrypt('swordfish', algorithm='AES-256') and says the argument implements a recommended modern encryption algorithm (Sweigart, 2025)(pypdf contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_EncryptAes256> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-EncryptAes256" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Protection .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:EncryptAes256 .
pg:DecryptReturnValues a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Decrypt return values"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter enters is_encrypted, then decrypt('an incorrect password').name, then decrypt('swordfish').name, and explains that decrypt() returns a PasswordType object whose name is NOT_DECRYPTED, USER_PASSWORD or OWNER_PASSWORD (Sweigart, 2025)(pypdf contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_DecryptReturnValues> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DecryptReturnValues" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Protection .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DecryptReturnValues .
pg:ReaderRefusesBeforeDecrypt a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Reader refuses before decrypt"@en ;
    skos:definition "Practice question 3 asks what to do before obtaining Page objects from an encrypted PdfReader, and the answer is to call decrypt() with the password (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_ReaderRefusesBeforeDecrypt> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ReaderRefusesBeforeDecrypt" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Protection .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ReaderRefusesBeforeDecrypt .
pg:EmptyUserPassword a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Empty user password"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter explains that a PDF can have a user password that allows viewing and an owner password that sets permissions for printing, commenting and extracting text (Sweigart, 2025). A reader opens such a file without being given a password."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_EmptyUserPassword> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-EmptyUserPassword" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Protection .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:EmptyUserPassword .
pg:ApiHistory a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Api history"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter uses PdfReader and PdfWriter and calls pages, append(), merge(), rotate(), add_blank_page(), insert_blank_page(), merge_page(), encrypt() and decrypt() (Sweigart, 2025). The pypdf history says the project began as pyPdf in 2005, was forked as PyPDF2 and merged back into pypdf, and the changelog records the removal of PdfMerger in 5.0.0 (pypdf contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_ApiHistory> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ApiHistory" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PdfWriting .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ApiHistory .
pg:RemovedNames a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Removed names"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter uses PdfReader and PdfWriter, and many tutorials use PdfFileReader, PdfFileWriter and PdfMerger, and the pypdf changelog records the removal of PdfMerger in 5.0.0 (Sweigart, 2025)(pypdf contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_RemovedNames> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RemovedNames" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ApiHistory .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RemovedNames .
pg:WriteNeedsBinaryFile a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Write needs binary file"@en ;
    skos:definition "Practice question 1 asks in what modes the file of a PdfWriter is opened to save the PDF, and the chapter's listings open files with 'wb' (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_WriteNeedsBinaryFile> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WriteNeedsBinaryFile" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ApiHistory .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WriteNeedsBinaryFile .
pg:WordDocuments a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Word documents"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Word section starts with the Python-Docx package, installed as python-docx and imported as docx, and with the three data types it uses: a Document object for the whole file, Paragraph objects for the paragraphs and Run objects for the stretches of text inside a paragraph (Sweigart, 2025)(Canny, 2013). Reading a document means looping over doc.paragraphs and, for a style, setting the style attribute to the name of a paragraph style or a character style (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_WordDocuments> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WordDocuments" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WordDocuments .
pg:DocxPackage a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Docx package"@en ;
    skos:definition "A Word document has the .docx extension; Python-Docx opens it with docx.Document(filename) and saves with save() (Sweigart, 2025)(Canny, 2013). The format is Office Open XML, a ZIP archive of XML parts, which the chapter does not show (Wikipedia, 2026)(Library of Congress, n.d.)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_DocxPackage> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DocxPackage" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WordDocuments .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DocxPackage .
pg:DocxIsZipOfXml a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Docx is zip of xml"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says .docx files have many structural elements which Python-Docx represents with three data types, and does not say what a .docx file is on disk (Sweigart, 2025). Office Open XML packages a document as a ZIP archive of XML parts, and a ZIP file usually starts with the bytes PK, the initials of its inventor Phil Katz (Library of Congress, n.d.)(Wikipedia, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_DocxIsZipOfXml> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DocxIsZipOfXml" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DocxPackage .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DocxIsZipOfXml .
pg:DocumentXmlBody a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Document xml body"@en ;
    skos:definition "The paragraphs, runs and text that the chapter names are elements of word/document.xml: w:p for a paragraph, w:r for a run and w:t for the text of a run (Library of Congress, n.d.)(Canny, 2013)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_DocumentXmlBody> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DocumentXmlBody" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DocxPackage .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DocumentXmlBody .
pg:DefaultTemplate a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Default template"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter creates a new document with docx.Document() and says the result has no paragraphs (practice question 10) (Sweigart, 2025). The python-docx documentation says a new document is created from a default template that the package ships (Canny, 2013)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_DefaultTemplate> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DefaultTemplate" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DocxPackage .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DefaultTemplate .
pg:ReadingText a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Reading text"@en ;
    skos:definition "Reading a Word document means opening it with docx.Document(), taking doc.paragraphs, a list of Paragraph objects, and for each paragraph its text and its list of Run objects; a new paragraph begins whenever the user presses ENTER, and a run is a stretch of text with one style (Sweigart, 2025)(Canny, 2013). The chapter's get_text() function joins the texts of the paragraphs (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_ReadingText> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ReadingText" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WordDocuments .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ReadingText .
pg:ParagraphsOfBody a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Paragraphs of body"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's get_text() function and its shell session read doc.paragraphs, a list of Paragraph objects, and join the text attribute of each; practice question 7 asks how to obtain that list (Sweigart, 2025)(Canny, 2013)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_ParagraphsOfBody> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ParagraphsOfBody" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ReadingText .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ParagraphsOfBody .
pg:RunsSplitAtFormatting a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Runs split at formatting"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says each Paragraph object contains a list of one or more Run objects and that the single-sentence paragraph of Figure 17-4 has four runs; it prints the run texts as 'A plain paragraph with some ', 'bold', ' and some ' and 'italic' (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_RunsSplitAtFormatting> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RunsSplitAtFormatting" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ReadingText .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RunsSplitAtFormatting .
pg:TablesAndHeadersApart a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Tables and headers apart"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says a Document object contains a list of Paragraph objects for the paragraphs in the document, and its get_text() function reads only those (Sweigart, 2025). The python-docx documentation puts tables in doc.tables and headers in the sections (Canny, 2013)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_TablesAndHeadersApart> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TablesAndHeadersApart" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ReadingText .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TablesAndHeadersApart .
pg:HyperlinkTextAndRuns a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Hyperlink text and runs"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says a paragraph is a list of runs and that its text attribute is the joined text of them (Sweigart, 2025). A hyperlink in Word is an element of its own that contains runs, and the python-docx documentation describes paragraph.runs as the runs directly in the paragraph (Canny, 2013)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_HyperlinkTextAndRuns> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-HyperlinkTextAndRuns" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ReadingText .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:HyperlinkTextAndRuns .
pg:StyleSystem a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Style system"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says Word documents contain three types of styles: paragraph styles apply to Paragraph objects, character styles to Run objects and linked styles to both; to style a paragraph or a run, set its style attribute to the name of the style, and for the linked style of a run add ' Char' to the name (Sweigart, 2025). It prints a list of default style names and says custom styles are made in the Word application (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_StyleSystem> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-StyleSystem" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WordDocuments .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:StyleSystem .
pg:StyleKindsOfTemplate a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Style kinds of template"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says Word documents contain three types of styles, paragraph, character and linked, and prints 34 default style names (Sweigart, 2025). The python-docx documentation distinguishes paragraph, character, table and numbering (list) styles (Canny, 2013)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_StyleKindsOfTemplate> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-StyleKindsOfTemplate" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:StyleSystem .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:StyleKindsOfTemplate .
pg:StyleKindMismatch a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Style kind mismatch"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says to set the style attribute of a Paragraph or Run to the name of the style, and that for a linked style a run uses the name with Char added, paragraphObj.style = 'Quote' and runObj.style = 'Quote Char' (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_StyleKindMismatch> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-StyleKindMismatch" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:StyleSystem .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:StyleKindMismatch .
pg:UnknownStyleKeyError a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Unknown style key error"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter prints the default style names in a table of strings, among them 'List Number ' with a space before the closing quote, and tells the reader to use those strings (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_UnknownStyleKeyError> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-UnknownStyleKeyError" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:StyleSystem .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:UnknownStyleKeyError .
pg:StyleIdLookupDeprecated a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Style id lookup deprecated"@en ;
    skos:definition "The default names the chapter prints include 'MacroText', which in the template is not a name (Sweigart, 2025). The python-docx documentation says styles are looked up by name and that a style also has an id (style_id) in the XML (Canny, 2013)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_StyleIdLookupDeprecated> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-StyleIdLookupDeprecated" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:StyleSystem .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:StyleIdLookupDeprecated .
pg:CustomParagraphStyle a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Custom paragraph style"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that to create custom styles one uses the Word application to define them and then reads them from the style attribute of a Paragraph or Run object; the Custom Invitations program asks the reader to add styles to a blank Word file first (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_CustomParagraphStyle> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CustomParagraphStyle" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:StyleSystem .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CustomParagraphStyle .
pg:WordWriting a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Word writing"@en ;
    skos:definition "Writing a Word document means calling add_paragraph() and add_run(), add_heading() with a level, add_break() with a break type on a run, and add_picture() with a width and a height, then save(); the chapter shows each in the shell and ends the section with the attributes of a run in Table 17-1 (Sweigart, 2025)(Canny, 2013). The document is saved to a .docx file that Word or LibreOffice Writer opens (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_WordWriting> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WordWriting" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WordWriting .
pg:BuildingContent a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Building content"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter creates a document with docx.Document(), adds a paragraph with add_paragraph('Hello, world!'), adds a run to it with add_run(), and adds headings with add_heading(text, level) (Sweigart, 2025)(Canny, 2013). Both add_paragraph() and add_run() accept an optional second argument that is a string of the style to use (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_BuildingContent> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BuildingContent" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WordWriting .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BuildingContent .
pg:AddParagraphReturnsParagraph a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Add paragraph returns paragraph"@en ;
    skos:definition "To write a document the chapter calls docx.Document() and then doc.add_paragraph('Hello, world!'), and saves it with doc.save('helloworld.docx'); Figure 17-7 shows the file (Sweigart, 2025)(Canny, 2013)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_AddParagraphReturnsParagraph> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AddParagraphReturnsParagraph" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BuildingContent .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AddParagraphReturnsParagraph .
pg:AddRunAppends a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Add run appends"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter adds a second paragraph and then a run to it, calling add_run() with the text to append, and notes that both add_paragraph() and add_run() accept an optional second argument for the style (Sweigart, 2025)(Canny, 2013). Figure 17-8 shows the result (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_AddRunAppends> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AddRunAppends" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BuildingContent .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AddRunAppends .
pg:AddHeadingLevels a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Add heading levels"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls add_heading('Header 0', 0) through level 4 and says the arguments are the heading text and an integer ranging from 0 to 4, where 0 is the Title style and the integers 1 to 9 are headings of decreasing size (Sweigart, 2025)(Canny, 2013). Practice question 12 asks which integers are the levels (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_AddHeadingLevels> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AddHeadingLevels" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BuildingContent .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AddHeadingLevels .
pg:RunFormatting a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Run formatting"@en ;
    skos:definition "Table 17-1 of the chapter lists twelve attributes of a Run object, bold, italic, underline, strike, double_strike, all_caps, small_caps, shadow, outline, rtl, imprint and emboss, and says each can be set to True, False or None, where None makes the run use the style's setting (Sweigart, 2025). The python-docx documentation puts the tri-state properties on the font of a run (Canny, 2013)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_RunFormatting> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RunFormatting" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WordWriting .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RunFormatting .
pg:TriStateBold a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Tri state bold"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says each text attribute can be set to True, always enabled, False, always disabled, or None, which defaults to whatever the style of the run says (Sweigart, 2025)(Canny, 2013)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_TriStateBold> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TriStateBold" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RunFormatting .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TriStateBold .
pg:UnderlineValues a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Underline values"@en ;
    skos:definition "The underline attribute is the one of the three Run attributes that takes more than True and False: the python-docx documentation says it accepts a member of the WD_UNDERLINE enumeration for the kinds of underline (Canny, 2013)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_UnderlineValues> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-UnderlineValues" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RunFormatting .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:UnderlineValues .
pg:RunVersusFontAttributes a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Run versus font attributes"@en ;
    skos:definition "Table 17-1 lists twelve attributes under the heading of text attributes that can be set on Run objects: bold, italic, underline, strike, double_strike, all_caps, small_caps, shadow, outline, rtl, imprint and emboss (Sweigart, 2025). Practice question 8 asks what type of object has bold, underline, italic, strike and outline variables (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_RunVersusFontAttributes> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RunVersusFontAttributes" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RunFormatting .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RunVersusFontAttributes .
pg:UnknownRunAttributeIgnored a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Unknown run attribute ignored"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's sentences about Table 17-1 tell the reader to set the attributes on Run objects, for instance runObj.strike = True (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_UnknownRunAttributeIgnored> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-UnknownRunAttributeIgnored" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RunFormatting .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:UnknownRunAttributeIgnored .
pg:BreaksAndPictures a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Breaks and pictures"@en ;
    skos:definition "To add a line break rather than a new paragraph the chapter calls add_break() on the Run object after which the break appears, and for a page break passes docx.enum.text.WD_BREAK.PAGE; add_picture() takes a filename and optional width and height, which can be given with docx.shared.Inches() and docx.shared.Cm() (Sweigart, 2025)(Canny, 2013)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_BreaksAndPictures> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BreaksAndPictures" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WordWriting .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BreaksAndPictures .
pg:PageBreakRun a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Page break run"@en ;
    skos:definition "To add a page break the chapter calls add_break() on the Run object after which the break should appear, passing docx.enum.text.WD_BREAK.PAGE, and describes a second page that begins with This is on the second page! (Sweigart, 2025)(Canny, 2013)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_PageBreakRun> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PageBreakRun" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BreaksAndPictures .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PageBreakRun .
pg:NewlineBecomesBreak a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Newline becomes break"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that to add a line break rather than a new paragraph one calls add_break() on a run (Sweigart, 2025). The python-docx documentation says that a newline or a tab in the text assigned to a run is turned into a break or a tab element (Canny, 2013)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_NewlineBecomesBreak> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-NewlineBecomesBreak" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BreaksAndPictures .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:NewlineBecomesBreak .
pg:PictureSizeFromWidth a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Picture size from width"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls doc.add_picture('zophie.png', width=docx.shared.Inches(1), height=docx.shared.Cm(4)) and says it is easier to give the width and height in inches and centimetres than in the units of the file (Sweigart, 2025)(Canny, 2013)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_PictureSizeFromWidth> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PictureSizeFromWidth" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BreaksAndPictures .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PictureSizeFromWidth .
pg:EmuUnits a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Emu units"@en ;
    skos:definition "docx.shared.Inches() and docx.shared.Cm() return lengths, and the python-docx documentation says lengths are integers in English Metric Units, 914,400 to the inch (Canny, 2013)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_EmuUnits> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-EmuUnits" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BreaksAndPictures .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:EmuUnits .
pg:PictureStoredOnce a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Picture stored once"@en ;
    skos:definition "Each add_picture() call adds a paragraph with a picture, and the chapter's own session adds one picture (Figure 17-9 shows headings and Figure 17-10 shows the invitation file) (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_PictureStoredOnce> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PictureStoredOnce" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BreaksAndPictures .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PictureStoredOnce .
pg:ToolsAndPractice a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Tools and practice"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter ends with a summary, twelve practice questions and three practice programs: PDF Paranoia encrypts and decrypts the PDFs of a folder tree, Custom Invitations makes one invitation per page from a list of guests, and PDF Password Breaker tries every word of a dictionary as the password of an encrypted PDF (Sweigart, 2025). Installing the two packages is left to Appendix A, which pins their versions (Sweigart, 2025)(Sweigart, 2025, Appendix A)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_ToolsAndPractice> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ToolsAndPractice" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ToolsAndPractice .
pg:PackagesAndSetup a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Packages and setup"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter tells the reader to install PyPDF and Python-Docx by following Appendix A, to install Python-Docx and not Docx, which belongs to a different package, and to import docx and not python-docx (Sweigart, 2025). Appendix A pins pypdf 5.0.1 and python-docx 1.1.2 (Sweigart, 2025, Appendix A)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_PackagesAndSetup> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PackagesAndSetup" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ToolsAndPractice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PackagesAndSetup .
pg:PackageNameTrap a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Package name trap"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says to install Python-Docx, not Docx, which belongs to a different package that the book does not cover, and to run import docx, not import python-docx (Sweigart, 2025). PyPI shows the project docx at version 0.2.4, released on 6 February 2014 (Python Package Index, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_PackageNameTrap> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PackageNameTrap" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PackagesAndSetup .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PackageNameTrap .
pg:OptionalDependencyAes a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Optional dependency aes"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says to follow Appendix A to install PyPDF (Sweigart, 2025)(Sweigart, 2025, Appendix A). The pypdf installation page says encryption with RC4 is supported by the regular installation, and AES needs the crypto extra, which installs the cryptography package (pypdf contributors, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_OptionalDependencyAes> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-OptionalDependencyAes" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PackagesAndSetup .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:OptionalDependencyAes .
pg:PracticePrograms a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Practice programs"@en ;
    skos:definition "Project 12 finds all PDF files in the working directory, sorts them alphabetically, copies every page after the first of each to an output PDF and saves it; the three practice programs encrypt and decrypt a folder tree of PDFs, make one invitation per page for each guest, and try the words of a dictionary as a password (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_PracticePrograms> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PracticePrograms" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ToolsAndPractice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PracticePrograms .
pg:CombineAllPdfs a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Combine all pdfs"@en ;
    skos:definition "Project 12 finds the PDF files of the working directory with os.listdir('.'), sorts them with pdf_filenames.sort(key=str.lower), creates a PdfWriter and, for each file, copies all pages after the first, then saves the output (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_CombineAllPdfs> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CombineAllPdfs" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticePrograms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CombineAllPdfs .
pg:CaseInsensitiveSort a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Case insensitive sort"@en ;
    skos:definition "Step 1 of Project 12 calls the sort() list method on pdf_filenames with the argument key=str.lower, so that the filenames are alphabetized without regard to case (Sweigart, 2025)(Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_CaseInsensitiveSort> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CaseInsensitiveSort" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticePrograms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CaseInsensitiveSort .
pg:WalkFindsAllPdfs a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Walk finds all pdfs"@en ;
    skos:definition "PDF Paranoia asks for os.walk() to go through every PDF in a folder and its subfolders, and Project 12 tests filename.endswith('.pdf') (Sweigart, 2025)(Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_WalkFindsAllPdfs> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WalkFindsAllPdfs" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticePrograms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WalkFindsAllPdfs .
pg:InvitationPageBreaks a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Invitation page breaks"@en ;
    skos:definition "Custom Invitations asks for one invitation per page for each guest, with styles added to a blank Word file (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_InvitationPageBreaks> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-InvitationPageBreaks" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticePrograms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:InvitationPageBreaks .
pg:DictionaryAttackBothCases a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Dictionary attack both cases"@en ;
    skos:definition "PDF Password Breaker reads a dictionary of words, passes each to decrypt(), and the chapter says to try both the uppercase and lowercase form of each word; it says the dictionary has over 44,000 words and the program makes about 88,000 attempts (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_DictionaryAttackBothCases> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DictionaryAttackBothCases" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticePrograms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DictionaryAttackBothCases .
pg:PracticeQuestions a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Practice questions"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter ends with twelve practice questions that ask for the modes in which the file of a PdfWriter is opened, how to get the Page object for page 5, what to do before getting pages from a PdfReader whose PDF is encrypted with the password swordfish, how to rotate a page counterclockwise, which method returns a Document for demo.docx, the difference between a Paragraph and a Run, how to get the list of paragraphs, which type of object has bold, underline, italic, strike and outline variables, what the values True, False and None of bold mean, how to create a new Document, how to add a paragraph and which integers are heading levels (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_PracticeQuestions> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PracticeQuestions" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ToolsAndPractice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PracticeQuestions .
pg:PracticeAnswersChecked a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Practice answers checked"@en ;
    skos:definition "The twelve questions ask for definitions, and some ask for code that can be run: the index of page 5, the call that returns a Document, the list of paragraphs, the variables of a Run and the heading levels (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch17#S_PracticeAnswersChecked> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PracticeAnswersChecked" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticeQuestions .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PracticeAnswersChecked .
